# Wave Optics: Interference, Diffraction, and Polarisation

> Light bends around razor blades, oil films glow with rainbow colours,
> and polaroid sunglasses cut glare from water. None of this makes sense
> if light were only a stream of particles. Wave optics explains what
> ray optics cannot — how light waves overlap, spread, and vibrate, and
> why these effects decide the design of telescopes, microscopes, and
> anti-glare coatings.

## 1. Huygens' Principle and the Nature of Wavefronts

[definition]
**Huygens' principle** states that every point on a wavefront acts as a
source of secondary wavelets that spread out in all directions with the
same speed as the wave. The new wavefront after a small time is the
tangent surface (envelope) drawn over all these secondary wavelets.
[/definition]

A wavefront is the locus of points vibrating in the same phase. Light
from a point source gives **spherical wavefronts**; far from the source
they flatten into **plane wavefronts**. Huygens' construction explains
reflection, refraction, and even the small bending of light near edges —
the first hint that shadows are never perfectly sharp.

| Phenomenon | What the wave model adds beyond rays |
| --- | --- |
| Interference | Two waves add to give bright and dark fringes |
| Diffraction | Light spreads into the geometric shadow |
| Polarisation | Light vibrates in a direction — proves transverse waves |
| Ray optics limit | When wavelength is negligible, rays suffice |

## 2. Coherent Sources and Superposition

For a stable interference pattern, the two sources must be
**coherent**: they should have the same frequency and a constant phase
difference. Two independent sodium lamps can never produce steady
fringes because their phase difference drifts randomly millions of
times per second. In Young's experiment, one slit is illuminated by
two virtual images of the same source, guaranteeing coherence.

[important]
Only **constructive interference** (path difference = whole multiples of
$\lambda$) produces bright fringes; **destructive interference**
(path difference = odd multiples of $\frac{\lambda}{2}$) produces dark
fringes. Intermediate path differences give intermediate brightness.
[/important]

@math label="eq:constructive"
\Delta L = n\lambda \quad (n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots) \quad \text{bright}
@/math

@math label="eq:destructive"
\Delta L = (2n+1)\frac{\lambda}{2} \quad (n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots) \quad \text{dark}
@/math

## 3. Young's Double-Slit Experiment

Light of wavelength $\lambda$ passes through two narrow slits separated
by distance $d$ and falls on a screen at distance $D$ (where
$D \gg d$). The path difference between the two routes to a point at
angle $\theta$ is $d\sin\theta \approx d\frac{y}{D}$, where $y$ is the
height of the point above the centre.

The **fringe width** — spacing between consecutive bright (or dark)
fringes — is the working formula of wave optics:

@math label="eq:fringe-width"
\beta = \frac{\lambda D}{d}
@/math

**Worked example.** Yellow light of $600\ \text{nm}$ illuminates slits
$0.3\ \text{mm}$ apart, with a screen $1.5\ \text{m}$ away:

@math label="eq:yds-example"
\beta = \frac{600 \times 10^{-9} \times 1.5}{0.3 \times 10^{-3}} = 3 \times 10^{-3}\ \text{m} = 3\ \text{mm}
@/math

[info title="Reading the formula"]
Fringe width grows with longer wavelength (red fringes are wider than
blue), grows with screen distance $D$, and shrinks with larger slit
separation $d$. If the whole apparatus is dipped in water, $\lambda$
shrinks by the refractive index and fringes crowd together.
[/info]

## 4. Thin-Film Interference and Colours of Films

A soap bubble or an oil slick shows colours because light reflected
from the top and bottom surfaces of the thin film travels different
distances and interferes. Since the film has slightly different
thickness at different points, different wavelengths interfere
constructively at different spots — painting the film with bands of
colour. This is why:

- Soap bubbles show shifting colours just before they burst (the film
  thins to a few hundred nanometres).
- Camera lenses are coated with a **quarter-wavelength layer** of
  magnesium fluoride so reflected waves from the two surfaces cancel —
  an anti-reflection coating.
- Wedge-shaped air films between glass plates produce straight,
  equally spaced fringes used to test optical flatness.

## 5. Diffraction: Light Enters the Shadow

[definition]
**Diffraction** is the bending and spreading of waves around obstacles
and apertures whose size is comparable to the wavelength. A single slit
of width $a$ sends light into the geometric shadow with intensity
minima at angles satisfying $a\sin\theta = n\lambda$.
[/definition]

The central maximum is twice as wide as the side maxima and carries
the bulk of the light. Notice the deep contrast with a double-slit
pattern: interference of two slits gives many equally bright fringes,
while diffraction of one slit gives a broad central band with weak
sidelobes.

**Resolving power.** Two point objects are just resolved when the
first diffraction minimum of one falls on the central maximum of the
other (Rayleigh criterion). For a lens or mirror of aperture $D$:

@math label="eq:rayleigh"
\theta_{min} = \frac{1.22\lambda}{D}
@/math

This single equation explains why large telescopes collect more light
*and* see finer detail, and why blue-tinted microscopes resolve smaller
features than red-tinted ones for the same aperture.

## 6. Polarisation: Light Has a Direction

Interference and diffraction happen for all waves; **polarisation
happens only for transverse waves**. Ordinary light vibrates in all
directions perpendicular to its travel; a **polariser** passes only
one vibration direction.

- **Reflection:** at Brewster's angle the reflected ray is completely
  polarised, and reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular:

@math label="eq:brewster"
\tan\theta_p = n
@/math

For glass ($n = 1.5$), $\theta_p \approx 57°$ — the basis of polaroid
sunglasses that kill water and road glare.

- **Transmission:** when polarised light passes through an analyser
  rotated by angle $\theta$ from its axis, the transmitted intensity
  follows **Malus's law**:

@math label="eq:malus"
I = I_0 \cos^2\theta
@/math

- **Double refraction:** crystals such as calcite split a beam into an
  ordinary ray and an extraordinary ray with different polarisations —
  the phenomenon through which most polarisers are made.

## 7. Exam Checklist and Common Traps

| Situation | Correct conclusion |
| --- | --- |
| Fringes crowd closer in water | $\lambda$ decreases, so $\beta$ decreases |
| White-light double slit | Central fringe white, coloured fringes with violet innermost |
| One slit closed in YDSE | No fringes — only single-slit diffraction |
| Slit width doubled (double slit) | Fringe positions unchanged; intensity changes |
| Brewster angle incidence | Reflected light fully polarised, refracted partially |
| Analyser parallel to polariser | $\theta = 0$, full transmission ($I = I_0$) |

[example]
Two polaroids are crossed (axes at $90°$), so no light passes
($I = I_0\cos^2 90° = 0$). Insert a third polaroid at $45°$ between
them. Surprisingly, light now gets through: the first pair of crossings
transmits $\frac{I_0}{2} \times \cos^2 45° = \frac{I_0}{4}$, and the
second crossing transmits $\frac{I_0}{4} \times \cos^2 45° = \frac{I_0}{8}$.
Three polaroids pass light where two do not — a favourite exam twist.
[/example]

[summary]
Wave optics handles what rays cannot. Coherent sources superpose to
give bright fringes at $\Delta L = n\lambda$ and dark fringes at
$(2n+1)\frac{\lambda}{2}$; Young's experiment yields the fringe width
$\beta = \frac{\lambda D}{d}$. Thin films colour soap bubbles, a single
slit diffracts with minima at $a\sin\theta = n\lambda$, and resolution
is limited by $\theta_{min} = \frac{1.22\lambda}{D}$. Polarisation —
governed by Brewster's law $\tan\theta_p = n$ and Malus's law
$I = I_0\cos^2\theta$ — proves light is a transverse wave. Together
these ideas set the limits of every optical instrument ever built.
[/summary]
